PAULI MATRICES  (spin-1/2 operators)
→ 530.12 → Quantum Mechanics

Three 2x2 complex Hermitian matrices
σx, σy, σz   (also written σ1, σ2, σ3)

σx = |  0   1 |
     |  1   0 |

σy = |  0  -i |
     |  i   0 |

σz = |  1   0 |
     |  0  -1 |

i = sqrt(-1)

FUNDAMENTALS
- Hermitian: σ† = σ
- Trace-zero: Tr(σi) = 0
- det(σi) = -1
- Eigenvalues: +1 and -1
- σi² = I   (I = 2x2 identity)

ALGEBRA
σi σj = δij I + i εijk σk

Example:
σx σy = i σz
σy σz = i σx
σz σx = i σy

and they anticommute when i ≠ j:
{σi, σj} = 2 δij I

Used for electron spin, magnetic moment,
and as the Pauli gates X, Y, Z in quantum computing.
